AmiNetXDuo -- TCP/IP for classic AmigaOS
========================================

AmiNetXDuo is a bsdsocket.library for AmigaOS 3.x: the shared library that
Amiga network software -- browsers, FTP clients, telnet, file sharing --
opens in order to reach the network. It talks to your network card through
the card's ordinary SANA-II driver, and it is configured with the same
files and the same commands as Roadshow.

It is built on Eclipse ThreadX NetX Duo and is MIT licensed throughout.


PLEASE READ THIS FIRST
----------------------

AmiNetXDuo works, and it is not finished.

It gets a DHCP lease, configures itself by SLAAC, answers ARP and
neighbour discovery, pings its gateway, resolves names and moves TCP in
both directions, and it scores 142 of 142 on the bsdsocktest conformance
suite. It has been tested under emulation on Kickstart 3.1 with a 68020
and a 68030, and on a real A3000 with an X-Surf-100.

Classic AmigaOS has no memory protection, so a fault in a network stack
takes the machine with it. Save your work before experimenting.


WHAT YOU NEED
-------------

  * Any 68000 or better, AmigaOS 3.1 or newer, about 4 MB of Fast RAM.
    The archive carries a separate build for the 68000, the 68020-68040
    and the 68060, and the Installer picks the right one. Encrypted
    (https:) connections need a 68020 or better.
  * A network card with a SANA-II driver installed in DEVS:
    (a2065.device, ariadne.device, x-surf.device, uaenet.device, ...)
    Drivers are not part of this archive; use the one for your card.
  * You need to know the driver's name and its unit number (usually 0).


INSTALLING
----------

Run the Installer script if there is one. To install by hand:

  copy bsdsocket.library LIBS:
  copy usergroup.library LIBS:
  copy AddNetInterface Online Offline ShowNetStatus netstat ping host TO C:
  makedir DEVS:NetInterfaces
  makedir DEVS:Internet

Do not run two TCP/IP stacks at once. If you already have AmiTCP, Miami or
Roadshow installed, rename the old LIBS:bsdsocket.library rather than
overwriting it, and make sure the old stack is not started from
S:User-Startup.


SETTING IT UP
-------------

Create one file per network card in DEVS:NetInterfaces. The name of the
file is the name of the interface. For a normal home network with a
router:

  DEVS:NetInterfaces/eth0
    DEVICE    = a2065.device
    UNIT      = 0
    CONFIGURE = DHCP

That is the whole configuration: the address, the netmask, the gateway and
the name servers all arrive from the DHCP server.

Note that the address mode keyword is CONFIGURE, not IPTYPE. IPTYPE with a
number after it means something else entirely (the SANA-II packet type)
and is ignored.

Without a DHCP server, use a fixed address instead:

  DEVS:NetInterfaces/eth0
    DEVICE    = a2065.device
    UNIT      = 0
    CONFIGURE = STATIC
    ADDRESS   = 192.168.1.10
    NETMASK   = 255.255.255.0

  DEVS:Internet/routes
    DEFAULT = 192.168.1.1

  DEVS:Internet/name_resolution
    nameserver 192.168.1.1

Then start the stack:

  AddNetInterface eth0

and, once that works, add this line to S:User-Startup so it happens at
every boot:

  C:AddNetInterface DEVS:NetInterfaces/eth0 QUIET

If you run a server on this machine -- an FTP daemon, a web server --
list it in DEVS:Internet/service_discovery and other machines on the
network will find it by browsing:

  DEVS:Internet/service_discovery
    _ftp._tcp     21
    _http._tcp    80    Amiga web server

Only list a server that is really running. There is a commented example
in the Examples drawer, and the guide has the full description.


IS IT WORKING?
--------------

  ShowNetStatus              is the stack up, and has it got an address?
  ping <your gateway>        can it reach the network?
  host www.aminet.net        do names work?

The first of those to fail is where the problem is. AmiNetXDuo.guide has a
troubleshooting section covering the usual causes: the wrong device name,
no DHCP server answering, a cable or driver problem, a missing interface
file, and a stack that is up while DNS is not.

From another machine on the same network, this Amiga also answers to its
own name with .local on the end -- "ping amiga.local" from a Mac, from
Linux with Avahi, or from Windows 10 or later. Nothing has to be set up
for that, and it keeps working when there is no DHCP server to hand out
an address anyone could guess.


THE COMMANDS
------------

  AddNetInterface   start the stack and bring an interface online
  Online / Offline  put an interface on or off the network
  ShowNetStatus     what the stack thinks the network looks like
  netstat           interfaces, routes and connections
  ping              is that machine there?
  host              look a name up, or an address back

All of them answer "?" with their arguments, in the usual AmigaDOS way.


DOCUMENTATION
-------------

AmiNetXDuo.guide is the manual. Double-click it, or:

  MultiView AmiNetXDuo.guide

It covers installation, every configuration file and keyword, all seven
commands with examples, and troubleshooting.


THINGS IT DOES NOT DO
---------------------

  * At most four interfaces are attached.
  * Only Ethernet-style SANA-II drivers have been tested; PPP and SLIP
    should be treated as unsupported.
  * A first HTTPS handshake to a busy host may not finish inside the far
    end's timeout on a 68020. It usually succeeds on the next attempt,
    because the session is cached. TLS is off on a 68000.
  * IPv6 multicast group membership is not available to programs.
  * The Roadshow interface-list, routing and statistics library calls
    return ENOSYS.

The guide has the full list with explanations.


